|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_change_in_f_

    Keeping $A$ as in Exercise >>_verbal_to_arithmetic_A_change_#decoy:55,
    and assuming that $A$ is differentiable at the point $t_0 \in \rr$, 
    how is

    $$
    { (\te{change in } A\te{ between } t = t_0 \te{ and } t = t_0 + r) \over r}
    $$

    written in “pure math”,
    and what does this fraction approach as $r$ approaches $0$?

    |> Solution

        The “pure math” rendition (rendering?) of the fraction
        is

        |> Boxed
            $$
            {A(t_0 + r) - A(t_0)\over r}
            $$

        which approaches

        |> Boxed
            $$
            A'(t_0)
            $$

        as $r$ approaches $0$, per Note >>_difference_quotient_limit_#decoy:2
        of Exercise >>_difference_quotient_illustration_#decoy:20. (This is a
        difference quotient, notwitstanding the renaming of “$h$” to “$r$”,
        which makes no difference\*.) (\*No pun intended.)